  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Scope for Linux**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the scope of the AI assistant for a Linux environment. The initial request was for \"systemwide\" functionality, but that\'s proving to be a complex challenge. I\'m exploring the limitations and potential areas where the AI assistant can operate effectively while respecting system security constraints. I need to clarify what\'s achievable with the `gemini-3.1-flash-live-preview` model and the available API.\n\n\n**Refining Architecture for Linux**\n\nI\'ve been iterating on the architecture for the Linux assistant. Originally, I thought a purely web-based solution, like an emulation, was the only way to go. But wait, maybe that is not the case? Now, I realize that because of Node.js, we can create a standard Vite + Express app that users can export. This setup allows the backend to execute system commands using `child_process.exec` when run locally, which is exciting! It means the assistant can have real system interactions.\n\n\n**Analyzing Security for Linux**\n\nI\'m now deeply concerned about the security implications. Building a Linux assistant that executes arbitrary shell commands based on voice input is a massive vulnerability. Even limiting it to \"safe\" commands presents risks. To mitigate this, I\'m pivoting to a full-stack React + Express app. I\'ll use the Gemini Live API for voice input and restrict the backend to read-only \"system\" tools like `getSystemInfo`, `getUptime`, `getDiskSpace`, and `getMemoryUsage`, utilizing `child_process.execSync`.\n\n\n**Developing Command Execution**\n\nI\'m now focusing on command execution within the Linux assistant. To meet the \"systemwide tasks\" request, I\'m leaning toward a `runCommand` tool on the backend, using `child_process.execSync`. I\'ll emphasize caution within the AI prompt, given the security risks. Running in AI Studio will limit scope. I will need to develop a `server.ts` to handle WebSockets and `ai.live.connect` and then have the frontend connect via WebSocket using `AudioContext` for microphone input.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 215200767
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
