  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Initiating Data Storage**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the new user request. Implementing the \"access past conversations\" feature is top priority. I\'m starting by designing a basic database to store conversations, ensuring seamless retrieval when initializing the Live API connection. This is the foundation for creating a memory component.\n\n\n**Considering Database Alternatives**\n\nI\'m now weighing database solutions. SQLite via `better-sqlite3` seems ideal for local storage, especially in a Linux environment. However, considering deployment, I\'m exploring a basic SQLite setup or a mock database to handle situations where local access isn\'t guaranteed. `better-sqlite3` is sufficient for a local assistant.\n\n\n**Refining Wake Word Integration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating a wake word feature. I\'m leaning towards using the Web Speech API\'s `webkitSpeechRecognition` to passively listen for a trigger word, initiating the Live API connection upon detection. This addresses the \"Always Listening\" or \"Wake Word Mode\" concept. I\'ve re-evaluated the scope, and the main features are now focused on wake word customization, background operation, complete memory access, custom modes via settings, and default modes. UI will have a settings modal/page, storing modes and history in `better-sqlite3`.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Local Storage**\n\nI\'ve decided on a JSON file, `nexus-db.json`, for storing modes and memory, prioritizing simplicity and avoiding potential compilation issues with `better-sqlite3` in the AI Studio environment. The server\'s API endpoints are now planned: `/api/config` for settings and wake word, and `/api/history` for conversation memory. I\'ll need to parse the Live API to get the user\'s input in real time.\n\n\n**Designing Settings UI**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the UI for settings. I\'m adding `lucide-react` icons for visual elements. A modal will house wake word customization, modes selection (Normal, Full, Custom), and an editor for custom modes. Background operation will use `webkitSpeechRecognition` for wake word detection, triggering the Live API. I\'m also planning to persist memory in `nexus-data.json`, injecting the recent history into the Live API via `systemInstruction`. Server modifications are underway to support this. I will look at tooling, such as `src`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 14
      nanos: 984685782
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
