    file_change_type: FILE_CHANGE_TYPE_CREATE
    replacement_chunks {
      replacement_content: "# Nexus OS Voice Assistant\n\nA system-wide AI voice assistant powered by the Gemini Live API, designed to run locally on your Linux machine with full command execution capabilities.\n\n## How to Run After Exporting\n\n**1. Extract the Project**\nUnzip the exported project to your desired directory.\n\n**2. Install Dependencies**\nEnsure you have [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) installed on your Linux system.\nOpen your terminal in the project directory and run:\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\n**3. Configure the Environment**\nDuplicate the `.env.example` file and rename it to `.env`:\n```bash\ncp .env.example .env\n```\nEdit the new `.env` file and insert your actual Gemini API Key:\n```env\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"your_actual_api_key_here\"\n```\n\n**4. Start the Assistant**\nTo start the fully featured local server, run:\n```bash\nnpm run dev\n```\n*(For a production-optimized build, run `npm run build` followed by `npm start`)*\n\n**5. Initialize the Core**\n- Open your web browser and navigate to **[http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000)**.\n- Click **\"Engage Audio Subsystem\"** to grant microphone permissions.\n- Once engaged, the system will begin listening for your configured wake word (default: \"nexus\") in the background.\n\n> **Note on Background Usage:** Keep the browser tab open (it can be pinned or minimized). The browser\'s Web Speech API requires an active tab to maintain background wake-word listening. When the wake word is detected, it will establish a direct websocket tunnel to Gemini Live and execute your verbal commands on your host Linux machine!"
    }
